00:02:18.524 --> 00:02:20.550
The idea of word2vec and others,

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.630
this is a machine learning process,

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.240
similar to other things that I've done

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.360
that looked at like, classification.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.150
Is this image a cat or a dog?

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.000
Or a regression analysis.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.120
What's the, what's, can you
predict the price of this house

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.010
based on certain properties of that house?

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:38.830
These are classic machine
learning examples.

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:43.030
Word2vec is a particular
machine learning model

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:46.440
that produces something
called a word embedding.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.480
Now, and that's a very, very fancy term.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.720
And what it means is that any given word,

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.540
like apple, can be associated

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:58.730
with numbers, a vector.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:01.690
This we can basically somehow come up

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:04.900
with this sort of like
numeric mathematical

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:09.540
essence of this word, as
some array of numbers.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.470
Like 0.7 and 1.2

00:03:12.972 --> 00:03:16.100
and -0.345, et cetera, et cetera.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.480
And there's going to be some
amount of numbers in here.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.680
This seems like a crazy thing.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:24.800
Why would I ever want to have a word

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.070
associated with an array of numbers?

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:29.440
Well one of the things that one can do

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.600
with arrays of numbers is math.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:35.080
Linear algebra, multiplying,

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.000
subtracting, averaging, adding.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.880
So, we know we can do that
with arrays of numbers.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.330
And this is the kind of thing that happens

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:45.960
in lots of my other tutorials
with programming graphics,

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:49.400
and pixel processing and machine learning.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.750
But one thing we wouldn't know how to do

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.993
is how would we say, you know,

00:03:55.467 --> 00:03:59.280
apple plus, I was going to say
plus orange, but that could be.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.840
I was trying to like come up
with something, a good example.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:02.860
This is what happens when you don't plan

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:04.220
these tutorials in advance.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:06.060
I could come up with
an example on the fly.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.523
Apple plus purple,

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:13.710
could this equal plum, maybe, right?

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:15.860
Like, in other words,
like, I'm trying to come up

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:17.890
with some like pseudo math.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.490
Like, let's take these two
words and add them together.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:24.309
like cat plus cute maybe
that equals kitten.

00:04:24.309 --> 00:04:26.630
And can I take, like,
we're not talking about

00:04:27.478 --> 00:04:28.311
concatenation, apple-purple.

00:04:28.311 --> 00:04:29.320
We're taking apple plus purple.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.760
Could I get that sort
of mathematical essence

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:34.830
of these words, add them
together and get a new word?

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:38.920
Well, the theory, the
prompt, the idea here,

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.770
the argument that I am making to you

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:42.990
is that word2vec is a mechanism.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.850
By which you can do stuff like this.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:47.010
Right there in your code.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.410
If I could quantify the word apple

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.694
as a series of numbers
and I could quantify

00:04:51.694 --> 00:04:55.073
the word purple as a series of numbers,

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:59.300
then couldn't I just add
all those numbers together?

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.270
I would get a new series of numbers.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:04.090
And then I might look and find which word,

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:06.981
or has a set of numbers that is most close

00:05:06.981 --> 00:05:08.850
to this set of numbers.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:11.000
How could I find the similarity?

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.510
I could calculate a similarity score

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:13.770
between any two sets of numbers.

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:15.830
I could find the word
that is the most similar

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:18.800
to this plus this and
maybe it would be plum.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.470
Why would it be plum?

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:21.490
Is that magic?

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:23.800
Is it because of what
data that's word2vec model

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:24.690
was trained on?

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.220
Well, yes, it's the latter.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.470
But, and so I want to get to all of that.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.600
Okay, this is my sort
of like zoomed out view

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:31.433
of why we're doing this.

00:05:31.433 --> 00:05:32.960
Let's come over and look at what Allison

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.380
has in her particular tutorial here.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:37.443
Which is a really nice example.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:40.130
If I look at this, we can say like,

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:42.480
well imagine like a
really simple case, right?

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.450
I was sort of saying, over here, each word

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:49.630
gets a list of maybe 100
numbers, maybe its 300 numbers,

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:51.010
maybe its 1000 numbers.

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:52.940
This is up to us to sort
of figure out and decide

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:54.130
based on what we're trying to do.

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:55.930
But what if we simplify that.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:57.670
And here's Alison's example.

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:01.020
Where each word gets
essentially two numbers.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:04.470
And those numbers are data
properties of that word.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.870
Like a cuteness score from zero to 100,

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:08.900
and a size from zero to 100.

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:11.520
So you could say kitten is 95, 15.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:13.406
A hamster is 80 comma eight, right?

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:15.070
There are these numbers that sort of like,

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:18.990
the label is tied to a
set of data properties.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:21.490
So if that's the case, then we can look,

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:23.870
we could graph all of those and
we could say something like,

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:26.180
oh, you know, like a horse and a dolphin

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:30.033
are kind of like similar in
terms of like size and cuteness.

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:33.300
And then we could start to do things by,

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.590
but actually like, we could
do a mathematical analysis.

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:37.820
Like what is the actual
Euclidean distance.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:40.230
Euclidean distance means the number of,

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:42.370
well in this case pixels or units,

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:45.580
between these two words right here.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:46.720
These are very similar because they're

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:48.180
physically close to each other.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:51.622
And we can also do things,
you can think of those as,

00:06:51.622 --> 00:06:53.870
and this is a nice
demonstration of this idea.

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:56.170
This is why we talk about
it as vectors, right?

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:59.550
I have a whole set of
tutorials about vectors,

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:03.910
describing as, describing points in space.

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:07.480
So, for example, a
vector, a velocity vector,

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.670
if I have a particle in a particle system,

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.400
and I want it to go from here to here,

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.770
this is its velocity.

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:17.170
Its change in location.

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:20.010
In essence, this is
basically what I'm doing

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:21.650
with an operation like this.

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:23.210
For example what if I said,

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:26.860
okay, well apple is over here.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:31.010
And then I'm going to add purple to it.

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:32.800
I'm going to move by purple's numbers,

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:35.220
and over here I now find plum.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.170
So when we look at this in two dimensions,

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:40.830
it kind of makes, we can sort of like,

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:42.230
our brains can understand that.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:44.530
Two dimensions is like
the easiest dimension.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:45.550
I mean I actually find two dimensions

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:47.060
to be easier than one dimension.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:48.830
One dimension is weird sometimes.

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:50.700
So, what Allison is showing here

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:52.410
is by moving from let's say,

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.000
one word to another word
physically in space,

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.160
we can establish this idea
of word relationships.

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.220
Chicken is to kitten as
tarantula is to hamster.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.200
Now this is all very arbitrary,

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.180
with like hard-coded word vectors.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:10.450
So, but this is just for
demonstration purposes

00:08:10.450 --> 00:08:12.170
in two dimensions so that our brains

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:13.720
can kind of process it.

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:18.180
Ultimately, if we have a lot
more information, somehow,

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:22.180
about all of these words in
higher dimensional space,

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:26.200
in vectors that have 100
dimensions, 100 numbers,

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:28.440
we can't visualize that so easily.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:29.763
There are interesting techniques

00:08:29.763 --> 00:08:31.503
called dimension reductionality.

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:36.620
Reducing the dimensionality
that we could then draw,

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:37.730
like word clusters and stuff.

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:39.380
And maybe I'll get to that later.

00:08:40.509 --> 00:08:42.810
But what I'm trying to say here

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:46.740
is that we can establish sophisticated

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:49.150
complex relationships between words

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:51.280
in higher dimensional space.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:53.280
But in order to do that it's useful

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.780
to look at a single
example that ties words

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:00.400
to numbers in a low dimensional space,

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:02.980
that we can either
visualize or if we like,

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:04.020
put into our brains.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.387
And so, I've kind of described to you

00:09:06.387 --> 00:09:08.400
what word2vec is.

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:10.600
What the model looks
like when it's complete.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:13.150
I haven't looked at all about
the training process, right?

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:15.010
The animals example is hard-coded.

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:16.440
I'm going to show you.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:19.140
I'm going to do a port of
one of Allison's examples

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:21.440
of words associated with colors associated

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:23.000
with numbers, right?

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:27.833
A word red is 255,0,0
that's a word to a vector.

00:09:27.833 --> 00:09:30.810
And that's going to be from a dataset.

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:32.950
And then the third thing
that I'm going to do

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:34.980
is look at what is traditionally thought

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:36.560
of as word2vec.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:40.920
These higher dimensional
large dictionaries

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:43.140
of words and their associated vectors.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:44.450
Those word embeddings.

